Your **thyroid** is a small gland in your neck. It makes hormones that control how fast your body uses energy. If you have an **underactive thyroid** (hypothyroidism), it does not make enough of these hormones, so many of your body's processes slow down.

It is common. In the UK it affects about **15 in every 1,000 women** and **1 in every 1,000 men**. It is most common in women over 60.

## Symptoms

Symptoms usually come on slowly, over months or years, and can be easy to mistake for other things:

- **tiredness** and feeling slow
- **weight gain**
- feeling **cold**
- **constipation**
- **dry skin** and **thinning hair**
- **aches and pains**, muscle cramps
- **low mood** or depression
- **poor memory** and concentration
- **heavy or irregular periods**
- a **slow heart rate**

## Causes

- **autoimmune thyroid disease** (Hashimoto's), where the immune system attacks the thyroid. This is the most common cause in the UK
- **treatment for an overactive thyroid**, such as radioactive iodine or surgery
- **some medicines**, such as lithium or amiodarone
- **radiotherapy** to the neck
- rarely, a problem with the **pituitary gland** in the brain

## Getting diagnosed

A simple **blood test** checks your levels of **TSH** (thyroid stimulating hormone) and **thyroxine (T4)**. A high TSH and low T4 usually means an underactive thyroid. Sometimes the test is repeated to be sure.

Some people have a slightly raised TSH with normal T4. This is called **subclinical hypothyroidism**. It does not always need treatment, but your GP may check it again.

## Treatment

Most people take a daily tablet called **levothyroxine**. See [hypothyroidism treatment](/conditions/physical/hypothyroidism/treatment/).

{% callout type="warn" title="Pregnancy" %}
If you have an underactive thyroid and are pregnant or planning a pregnancy, **tell your GP straight away**. Your dose usually needs to go up early in pregnancy.
{% /callout %}
